## F3. The cause: OPTICS's minimum cluster size

Today's DIALS replayed from each trial's saved correlation matrix with one stage changed at a time
(`cf/cause.py`, DIALS's own functions): the minimum-samples floor of the OPTICS optimisation (5, below
which no cluster can be reported) lowered to 2; the dimension count raised by one or fixed at 5; the
paper's fixed heuristic instead of the search; coordinates scaled to unit length.

Cryo:

{{ cause_table(n, C) }}

Room temperature (the floor alone, the only stage that mattered on cryo: replayed at full data, 60 degrees,
for minorities up to six and the single-population trials there):

{{ cause_table(n, R) }}

Only the floor matters: with it at 2, small minorities are reported at full data, and so are groups in
single-population data (the false-group rate rises from {{ n[C ~ '|cause'].default.false_groups.values()|map('first')|sum }}
to {{ n[C ~ '|cause'].floor2.false_groups.values()|map('first')|sum }} of {{ n[C ~ '|cause'].floor2.false_groups.values()|map('last')|sum }}
on cryo): OPTICS's density criterion has no notion of how far a group stands out of the noise, so the
floor is what keeps it from reporting noise and structure inside one species. The minority is already
separated in the coordinates OPTICS receives at full data (cryo, `cf/cause.py`):

{{ separability_table(n, C) }}

At less data per crystal the coordinates no longer keep the minority together (nearest-neighbour
purity falls), the counting noise of the pairwise correlations (F5) taking over.

## F4. A procedure that finds smaller groups, and its held-out judgements
{%- set V6 = n.preregistered %}{% set V7 = n.floor_only %}{% set V8 = n.precise %}{% set V9 = n.snr %}{% set V10 = n.takeout %}{% set J = n.judged %}

**{{ V6 }}** (`dials-patch/0001-0007`; the same decisions in 0001-0009, which fix a failure found end to
end and make the bootstrap faster; parameters in `dials-patch/frozen-v6.phil`): after OPTICS, a detector
works on DIALS's own pairwise correlation matrix. It fits one population (rank-1, as cosym in one
dimension) and scans the residuals for the group of data sets that stands out most against counting
noise (look-elsewhere over groups and sizes, p < 0.01). A significant group is reported when it is
separated from the remaining population by more than that population's own strongest structure (3.5
times), or when its coherence exceeds 0.05 x 2/k for a group of k. The scan is run twice, with two models
of the counting noise of every correlation: the large-sample error of Fisher's z with the pair's
effective number of reflections (pairs then need p < 0.001), and a bootstrap over reflections, which
discounts correlations resting on a few strong reflections; the groups of the first run, and those of the
second that share no data set with them, are taken out of the OPTICS clusters. Developed on cryo and
frozen in `PREREGISTRATION.md` amendment 12 (tag `frozen-v6`) before any room-temperature result of any
new procedure: **its room-temperature numbers are the pre-registered held-out judgement.**

