## F6. What limits {{ P }}: {{ 'the data' if not gx else 'the data, except in ' ~ gx|length ~ ' cell(s)' }}

Every minority {{ P }} does not find is classified by the measurement that settles it
(`cf/limits3.py`, amendments 4, 6, 7, 10, 11, 13, 14, 18, 24). It is judged together with its sub-groups that
would count as finding it (at least half of its members, and two): for each size, the sub-group with
the largest block statistic. **Counting noise**: neither the minority nor any of these sub-groups stands
out of the repeat-calibrated counting noise at 0.001, by the standardised block sum or the
inverse-variance weighted sum of its pairs, against the noise's largest group over all groups and all
sizes (a procedure does not know the members or their number); a procedure that reported it would report
groups from noise alone in more than one single-population trial in a thousand, above the false-group
rate of today's DIALS on cryo (0 of 189). **Structure inside one species**: every one of them that
stands out of the noise is matched by a group of crystals of one species of its size, in a
single-population trial of the same collection with as much or more data per crystal (or in the trial's
own majority), that stands out of the noise and is at least as coherent and as separated, within two
standard errors: a procedure that decides by a group's coherence and separation, as every procedure
here does, and reported the minority would, within the sampling error of both, report that group in
single-population data too (the limits without that tolerance are given below). **Noise known only from
repeat measurements**: it stands out of the repeat-calibrated noise but not of the noise the experiment
can establish for itself without repeats. **Membership not resolved** (amendment 15): it
would otherwise be separable, but a search that does not know the members finds, with either statistic,
a more significant group that mixes it with majority crystals and would not count as finding it: the data
favour a grouping that is not the minority. Otherwise **procedure**. A minority found by any frozen
procedure that reports no more false groups than today's DIALS is never a data limit. Single-population
groups of every size from two to half the trial are compared (amendment 24).

