## F7. Structure inside one species is as strong as a species difference

The strongest groups of crystals of one species are real and reproducible: human X1, X5 and X13 (the
largest cells and most strong spots of the human crystals) and porcine 6, 12, 14, 15 and 16 (small
cells) stand out of the counting noise in single-population trials at every data amount where a species
difference does; at small wedges single pairs (porcine PIX5 with PIX8, PIX13 or PIX7) stand out as
strongly as a pair of another species (coherence 0.07-0.095 at 0.5 degrees, against 0.06-0.16 for
species pairs). Relative to the rest of their species they are as distinct as a species minority is
relative to a porcine majority; only the size of the difference separates them at full data, which is
why {{ P }} uses a coherence threshold beside the separation ratio and why, at small wedges, these
groups set the limit (F6).

## F8. Room-temperature crystals of one species hold sub-groups as distinct as a species

In the data-quality pilot (before any trial), at full data the species differ in pairwise correlation by
about 0.03 (bovine-porcine), 0.02 (bovine-human) and 0.005 (human-porcine) at room temperature, against
0.05-0.09 at cryo (`results/pilot`). And the room-temperature crystals of one species are not one
population: the paper notes "clear substructures within the porcine and human insulin groups"
(section 3.2.2), and today's DIALS splits them in {{ totals(n, R, 'today').false_groups[0] }} of the {{ totals(n, R, 'today').false_groups[1] }} room-temperature
single-population trials.
